## Practical 1: Light Microscopy and Cell Identification

### Objective
- To learn the basic principles and proper use of a light microscope.
- To observe and identify cells and cellular structures.
- To understand magnification, resolution, and field of view.

### What to Learn
- Magnification
- Resolution
- Objective lens
- Eyepiece
- Focusing
- Field of view
- Bright-field microscopy

### Practical Work
- Identify the major parts of a microscope.
- Prepare or obtain a suitable cell/tissue slide.
- Properly place and focus a slide.
- Use low-, medium-, and high-power objectives.
- Calculate and Compare cell size, shape, and arrangement.
- Record observations.

### Expected Outcome
- Proper microscope handling
- Slide observation
- Basic cell identification
- Morphological description

---

## Practical 2: Slide Preparation

### Objective
- To learn how to prepare a biological specimen for microscopic observation.

### What to Learn
- Sample collection
- Slide preparation
- Coverslip placement
- Basic staining
- Prevention of air bubbles and contamination

### Practical Work
- Place an appropriate biological sample on a clean slide.
- Add the required stain or mounting solution.
- Place the coverslip properly.
- Observe the prepared slide under the microscope.
- Record cellular morphology.

### Expected Outcome
- Proper slide preparation
- Basic staining technique
- Microscopic observation
- Sample handling

---

## Practical 3: Cell Counting and Cell Concentration

### Objective
- To determine the concentration and approximate number of cells in a sample.
- To develop accurate quantitative cell-counting skills.

### What to Learn
- Cell concentration
- Dilution factor
- Counting chamber
- Cells/mL
- Replicate counting

### Practical Work
- Prepare an appropriate cell suspension.
- Load the sample into a counting chamber.
- Count cells in the designated areas.
- Apply the appropriate dilution factor.
- Calculate cell concentration.
- Record the result.

### Expected Outcome
- Accurate cell counting
- Dilution calculation
- Quantitative data recording
- Basic biological calculations

---

## Practical 4: Aseptic Technique

### Objective
- To learn how to prevent contamination during laboratory work.
- To maintain sterile conditions while handling biological samples.

### What to Learn
- Aseptic technique
- Sterility
- Contamination
- Sterile workspace
- Sterile materials
- Biosafety

### Practical Work
- Prepare a clean working area.
- Handle sterile materials correctly.
- Open and close containers without contamination.
- Minimize exposure of samples to the environment.
- Dispose of biological materials appropriately.

### Expected Outcomes
- Able to apply basic aseptic techniques.
- Able to recognize and prevent common sources of contamination.

---

### Practical 5: Pipetting

### Objective
- To learn accurate and precise liquid handling using micropipettes.

### What to Learn
- Micropipette types
- Volume range
- Accuracy
- Precision
- Pipette tips
- First and second stop
- Pre-wetting
- Pipetting errors

### Practical Work
- Select the appropriate micropipette.
- Set the correct volume.
- Attach and discard tips properly.
- Aspirate and dispense liquids correctly.
- Avoid bubbles and contamination.
- Perform repeated measurements consistently.

### Expected Outcome
- Able to use common micropipettes correctly.
- Able to transfer small liquid volumes accurately and reproducibly.


### Practical 6 — Serial Dilution

### Objective
- To learn how to prepare a series of known sample dilutions.
- To understand dilution factors and their applications.

### What to Learn
- Dilution
- Serial dilution
- Dilution factor
- Concentration
- Stock solution
- Working solution

### Practical Work
- Prepare a dilution series.
- Calculate dilution factors.
- Perform accurate pipetting during dilution.
- Calculate the final concentration.
- Organize dilution steps systematically.

### Expected Outcome
- Able to prepare serial dilutions accurately.
- Able to calculate dilution factors and final concentrations.

